 ### Bilinear Interpolation with Sigmoid Scaling
-A spatial color variation function that uses bilinear interpolation to model intra-primitive color changes, scaled by a sigmoid function to handle coordinates outside the standard (0,1) range. Coordinates $(u, v)$ are passed through a sigmoid function before bilinear sampling: $u' = \sigma(u), v' = \sigma(v)$. This maps arbitrary coordinates to (0,1) but compresses gradients near quadrant centers. Sigmoid scaling causes pixel gradients to approach zero near the center of a quadrant, leading to gradient vanishing during sharp color transitions and preventing perfect scene fitting. Bilinear interpolation is only effective when the spatial color pattern conforms to a four-quadrant distribution. It captures abrupt color transitions more effectively than tiny neural networks or movable kernels, but suffers from vanishing gradients in other regions, reducing overall expressiveness when multiple Gaussians are superimposed [S15].
